# the argument of hume-induction.md by rule (phil/skeleton): connectives from the parse, an atom to each clause
logic	s5
atom	divid_two_kind(x)	x: may be divided into two kinds namely demonstrative reasoning or that concerning relations of ideas and moral reasoning or that concerning matter of fact and existence
atom	reason(x)	x: reasonings
atom	demonstrative_argument_case	that there are no demonstrative arguments in the case seems evident
atom	impli_contradiction_course	it implies contradiction that the course of nature may change
atom	seemingly_experienc_attend(x)	x: seemingly like those which we have experienced may be attended with different or contrary effects
atom	object(x)	x: and that object
atom	clearly_distinctly_conceive	may I not clearly and distinctly conceive that a body falling from the clouds and which in all other respects resembles snow has yet the taste of salt or feeling of fire ?
atom	intelligible_proposition_affirm(x)	x: more intelligible proposition than to affirm that all the trees will flourish in December and January and decay in May and June
atom	now_intelligible(x)	x: Now is intelligible
atom	distinctly_conceiv	can be distinctly conceived
atom	impli_contradiction	implies contradiction
atom	prov_false_demonstrative	can be proved false by any demonstrative argument or abstract reasoning à priori
atom	engag_argument_put	if we be therefore engaged by arguments to put trust in past experience and make it the standard of our future judgement these arguments be probable only or such as regard matter of fact and real existence according to the division above mentioned
atom	appear_explication_speci(x)	x: but that there is must appear if our explication of that species of reasoning be admitted as solid and satisfactory
atom	argument_kind(x)	x: argument of this kind
atom	said_argument_concern	we have said that all arguments concerning existence are founded on the relation of cause and effect that our knowledge of that relation is derived entirely from experience
atom	experimental_conclusion_proce	that all our experimental conclusions proceed upon the supposition that the future will be conformable to the past
atom	evidently_going_circle(x)	x: to endeavour therefore be evidently going in a circle
atom	tak_grant_point	taking that for granted which is the very point in question
P1	S1	(all x: reason(x) -> divid_two_kind(x))
P2	S2	demonstrative_argument_case
P3	S2	(!impli_contradiction_course & (!impli_contradiction_course -> (all x: object(x) -> seemingly_experienc_attend(x))) & (all x: object(x) -> seemingly_experienc_attend(x)))
P4	S3	clearly_distinctly_conceive
P5	S4	(all x: intelligible_proposition_affirm(x) -> true(x))
P6	S5	(all x: now_intelligible(x))
P7	S5	(distinctly_conceiv & !impli_contradiction & !prov_false_demonstrative)
P8	S6	[]engag_argument_put
P9	S7	(all x: argument_kind(x) -> !appear_explication_speci(x))
P10	S8	said_argument_concern
P11	S8	experimental_conclusion_proce
P12	S9	[]evidently_going_circle(proof_last_supposition)
C	S9	tak_grant_point
# rules tool=phil/skeleton date=2026-10-20
